@startuml
' ============================================================================
' METADATA
'   author:       TheRobotDrafts Examples
'   description:  Variant contrasting the same small order-processing subsystem
'                 rendered in Yourdon/DeMarco notation vs Gane-Sarson notation,
'                 with a note on the visual and structural differences.
'   usecase:      Teaching that DFDs have two dominant notations (Yourdon and
'                 Gane-Sarson) that convey the SAME semantics with different
'                 shape conventions, and how to read each.
'   complexity:   simple
'   domain:       e-commerce / order processing (teaching variant)
' ============================================================================

title Data Flow Variant - Yourdon vs Gane-Sarson

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' TEACHING NOTES
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' Two notations dominate DFDs. They are SEMANTICALLY IDENTICAL — the same
' diagram in a different visual dialect.
'
'                       Yourdon / DeMarco       Gane / Sarson
'   Process             circle (bubble)         rounded rectangle
'                       numbered inside         with an ID box
'   Data Store          open-ended rectangle    two horizontal lines
'                       (two lines, number Dn)  (open box, number Dn)
'   External Entity     square                  square (often 4-corner box)
'   Flow                labeled arrow           labeled arrow (same)
'
' Read the SAME: data flows from sources through processes to stores/sinks.
' Pick the notation your team/toolchain expects; do not mix the two in one DFD.
'
' In this file we approximate Gane-Sarson's rounded process with a rectangle
' styled to read as rounded, and use the same storage shape — PlantUML has no
' native Gane-Sarson shapes, so the converter-to-.trd-yaml carries the notation
' tag. The visual cue is the process shape: circle vs rounded-rect.
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

' ============================
' LEFT: YOURDON / DeMARCO
' ============================
package "Yourdon / DeMarco" {
  rectangle "Customer"      as YC <<externalEntity>>
  usecase  "1\nProcess\nOrder" as YP1 <<process>>
  storage  "Orders\n(D1)"   as YD1 <<dataStore>>

  YC  --> YP1 : order
  YP1 --> YD1 : order record
  YP1 --> YC  : receipt
}

note left of YP1
  **Yourdon shapes**
  circle  = Process (number inside)
  storage = open-ended rectangle
  square  = External entity
end note

' ============================
' RIGHT: GANE-SARSON
' ============================
package "Gane-Sarson" {
  rectangle "Customer"            as GC <<externalEntity>>

  ' Gane-Sarson process = rounded rectangle with an ID box on the edge.
  ' We approximate the rounded-rect + ID box using a rectangle styled in a note.
  rectangle "1.0\nProcess Order"  as GP1 <<process>>
  storage  "D1  Orders"           as GD1 <<dataStore>>

  GC  --> GP1 : order
  GP1 --> GD1 : order record
  GP1 --> GC  : receipt
}

note right of GP1
  **Gane-Sarson shapes**
  rounded rect = Process (ID in a corner box)
  two-line box = Data store (numbered D1, D2)
  square       = External entity (often 4-corner)
  --
  Semantically IDENTICAL to Yourdon — only
  the visual dialect differs. Pick one and
  use it consistently within a diagram set.
end note

note bottom of GD1
  **How to choose**
  - Yourdon/DeMarco: common in US software-engineering texts and CASE tools.
  - Gane-Sarson:    common in enterprise/legacy analysis and some European tooling.
  - Rule: never mix notations in a single DFD. The converter tag
    (yourdon vs gane-sarson) drives the rendered shape set.
end note
@enduml
